e top of the function and replaced the `.some` scan with `insuredCounts.has(damage.itemType)` — a single source of truth for \"is this type insured,\" reused by both the per-item check and the per-type count check.\n\n## APP Mass — After\n\n```ts\nconst insuredCounts = countByType(policy.items);\nfor (const damage of damages) {\n  if (damage.amount < 0) { throw ... }\n  if (!insuredCounts.has(damage.itemType)) { throw ... }\n}\nconst damagedCounts = countByType(damages.map((d) => ({ type: d.itemType })));\nfor (const [type, damagedCount] of damagedCounts) { ... }\n```\n\n- Loops: 3 (`for...of` damages, `for...of` damagedCounts, `.map`) → 15\n- Conditionals: 2 → 8\n- Bindings: `insuredCounts`, `damage`, `damagedCounts`, `type`, `damagedCount`, `d` → 6\n- Invocations: `.has`, `countByType`×2, `.map` → 4 × 2 = 8\n- Constants: `0` → 1\n\n**Total ≈ 38**\n\n## Refactoring Complete\n\n**Refactoring**: Eliminated the `.some()` linear scan for \"is item type insured\" by reusing the already-necessary `insuredCounts` Map (built once, hoisted to the top of `validateDamages`), replacing it with an O(1) `Map.has()` lookup. This removes duplicated knowledge about the policy's insured types (previously computed two different ways) and drops one loop.\n\n**Mass Change**: 45 → 38 (Δ -7)\n\n**Tests**: All 26 passing (5/5 in cli.spec.ts), verified via `NODE_ENV=development pnpm test`."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-sonnet-5@eu","usage":{"input":5125,"output":1320,"cacheRead":10090,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":16535,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1784919527774,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-4e9a183e-19f2-4323-b72d-261c06e6bc86","responseModel":"claude-sonnet-5"}],"stderr":"","usage":{"input":17744,"output":9568,"cacheRead":49484,"cacheWrite":0,"cost":0,"contextTokens":16535,"turns":5},"model":"requesty/vertex/claude-sonnet-5@eu","stopReason":"stop"}]},"isError":fal