ut AND tracks/enforces an insurance cap across claims (given the new `capAlreadyUsed` context and `remainingCap` output)\n- Question: Does `calculatePayout` still capture this? It also computes the cap and returns `remainingCap`, but the name is still accurate as the primary output is the payout, and `ClaimResult`/`ClaimContext` types already communicate the cap-tracking aspect.\n- Assessment: Name still fits — the cap logic is a supporting mechanism for computing payout correctly, not a separate concern from the caller's perspective.\n\n**Decision: Keep `calculatePayout`** — it still accurately reflects the function's primary responsibility; the cap fields are implementation details exposed via well-named types (`ClaimContext.capAlreadyUsed`, `ClaimResult.remainingCap`).\n\n## APP Mass Calculation (Before)\n\nThe insurance-sum/cap block was inlined:\n```ts\nconst insuranceSum = input.policy.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.insuranceValue, 0);\nconst cap = insuranceSum * CAP_MULTIPLIER;\n```\n- Bindings: `insuranceSum`, `cap`, `sum`, `item` = 4\n- Invocations: `reduce` = 1 → 2\n- Loops: `reduce` = 1 → 5\n- Constants: `0` = 1\n- Assignments: 0 (declarations, not counted as mutation here)\n\nLocal mass for this block ≈ 4 + 2 + 5 + 1 = 12 (embedded inline in the main function body)\n\n## Rule Evaluation\n\n- **Rule 1 (Tests Pass)**: All 9 active tests passing before and after ✓\n- **Rule 2 (Reveals Intent)**: The inline insurance-sum/cap computation mixed a distinct sub-calculation (\"what's the maximum this policy will ever pay out\") into the main flow of `calculatePayout`. Extracting it into `calculateInsuranceCap(policy)` names this concept explicitly and separates \"compute the cap\" from \"compute this claim's payout against the cap.\"\n- **Rule 3 (No Duplication)**: No duplication found elsewhere.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: One new top-level function added — justified because it gives a previously anonymous calculation a name and a clear boundary (it will also be relevant for the still-`it.todo` tests about cap calculation with unmodified insurance values and block discounts).\n\n## APP Mass Calculation (After)\n\n`calculateInsuranceCap`:\n```ts\nconst calculateInsuranceCap = (policy: Policy): number => {\n  const insuranceSum = policy.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.insuranceValue, 0);\n  return insuranceSum * CAP_MULTIPLIER;\n};\n```\n- Bindings: `policy`, `insuranceSum`, `sum`, `item` = 4\n- Invocations: `reduce` = 1 → 2\n- Loops: `reduce` = 1 → 5\n- Constants: `0` = 1\n\nFunction-local mass = 4 + 2 + 5 + 1 = 12 (unchanged — code just moved, not simplified numerically)\n\nIn `calculatePayout`, the two lines collapse to one invocation:\n```ts\nconst cap = calculateInsuranceCap(input.policy);\n```\n- Bindings: `cap` = 1\n- Invocations: `calculateInsuranceCap` = 1 → 2\n\nMain-function-body mass for this concern: 1 + 2 = 3 (down from 12 inline)\n\n**Mass Change (main function body)**: 12 → 3 (delta -9), with the 12 units of complexity now isolated in a well-named helper instead of embedded in the main flow.\n\n## Refactoring Applied\n\n- **Extracted** `calculateInsuranceCap(policy: Policy): number` from the inline insurance-sum/cap calculation in `calculatePayout`.\n- **Rationale**: This calculation represents a distinct domain concept (the policy's payout ceiling) that was previously anonymous and mixed into the claim-payout flow. Naming it improves Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) and will make it easy to extend correctly when the pending `it.todo` tests about cap-based-on-unmodified-insurance-value and block-discount insurance sums are implemented later.\n- **Naming**: Kept `calculatePayout` as-is — still accurate.\n\nRefactoring Complete:\n**Refactoring**: Extracted `calculateInsuranceCap` helper function from inline cap calculation logic\n**Mass Change**: Main function body concern: 12 → 3 (moved into named helper, total system mass unchanged but now organized by concept)\n**Tests**: All 9 active tests passing (3 `it.todo` remain, unaffected)"}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_17-51-45_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_sonnet-5-no-thinking/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: src/claim.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/claim.ts\nPassing tests: 5\nRecent Green phase: Added an optional second parameter `context: ClaimContext = {}` to calculatePayout with a `capAlreadyUsed` field. Computed insuranceSum (sum of policy items' insuranceValue), cap = insuranceSum * 2, capRemainingBeforeClaim = cap - capAlreadyUsed. Final payout is Math.min(desiredPayout, capRemainingBeforeClaim), and remainingCap = capRemainingBeforeClaim - payout.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. Note: NODE_ENV must be set to \"development\" for `pnpm test` to work in this sandbox, e.g. `NODE_ENV=development pnpm test`.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done.","exitCode":0,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Task: Test file: src/claim.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/claim.ts\nPassing tests: 5\nRecent Green phase: Added an optional second parameter `context: ClaimContext = {}` to calculatePayout with a `capAlreadyUsed` field. Computed insuranceSum (sum of policy items' insuranceValue), cap = insuranceSum * 2, capRemainingBeforeClaim = cap - capAlreadyUsed. Final payout is Math.min(desiredPayout, capRemainingBeforeClaim), and remainingCap = capRemainingBeforeClaim - payout.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. Note: NODE_ENV must be set to \"development\" for `pnpm test` to work in this sandbox, e.g. `NODE_ENV=development pnpm test`.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done."}],"timestamp":1784918326581},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01JEq2VCEi7ayvAg2LqAGGef","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01FhH3