main flow of `calculatePayout`. Extracting it into `calculateInsuranceCap(policy)` names this concept explicitly and separates \"compute the cap\" from \"compute this claim's payout against the cap.\"\n- **Rule 3 (No Duplication)**: No duplication found elsewhere.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: One new top-level function added — justified because it gives a previously anonymous calculation a name and a clear boundary (it will also be relevant for the still-`it.todo` tests about cap calculation with unmodified insurance values and block discounts).\n\n## APP Mass Calculation (After)\n\n`calculateInsuranceCap`:\n```ts\nconst calculateInsuranceCap = (policy: Policy): number => {\n  const insuranceSum = policy.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.insuranceValue, 0);\n  return insuranceSum * CAP_MULTIPLIER;\n};\n```\n- Bindings: `policy`, `insuranceSum`, `sum`, `item` = 4\n- Invocations: `reduce` = 1 → 2\n- Loops: `reduce` = 1 → 5\n- Constants: `0` = 1\n\nFunction-local mass = 4 + 2 + 5 + 1 = 12 (unchanged — code just moved, not simplified numerically)\n\nIn `calculatePayout`, the two lines collapse to one invocation:\n```ts\nconst cap = calculateInsuranceCap(input.policy);\n```\n- Bindings: `cap` = 1\n- Invocations: `calculateInsuranceCap` = 1 → 2\n\nMain-function-body mass for this concern: 1 + 2 = 3 (down from 12 inline)\n\n**Mass Change (main function body)**: 12 → 3 (delta -9), with the 12 units of complexity now isolated in a well-named helper instead of embedded in the main flow.\n\n## Refactoring Applied\n\n- **Extracted** `calculateInsuranceCap(policy: Policy): number` from the inline insurance-sum/cap calculation in `calculatePayout`.\n- **Rationale**: This calculation represents a distinct domain concept (the policy's payout ceiling) that was previously anonymous and mixed into the claim-payout flow. Naming it improves Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) and will make it easy to extend correctly when the pending `it.todo` tests about cap-based-on-unmodified-insurance-